WEBVTT

00:00.800 --> 00:01.423
Welcome.

00:01.723 --> 00:06.967
In this video we'll build a systematic mental model of the post-training tool ecosystem,

00:06.987 --> 00:09.838
the way a senior post-training engineer sees it.

00:10.138 --> 00:14.126
Not a list of frameworks, but a layered map: what each layer computes,

00:14.146 --> 00:18.534
what math it must preserve, and how the pieces compose into a working system.

00:19.404 --> 00:23.983
Everything we discuss comes back to three forces that pull against each other:

00:24.003 --> 00:26.341
how freely you can change the algorithm,

00:26.361 --> 00:28.936
how much engineering complexity you take on,

00:28.956 --> 00:31.530
and how efficiently the whole loop executes.

00:31.830 --> 00:35.481
The material comes from the two research reports in this folder,

00:35.501 --> 00:40.758
one of which reviews the source code of the major frameworks at their October 2026 releases.

00:42.428 --> 00:43.415
Here's the roadmap.

00:43.715 --> 00:48.756
Part one derives a single equation that covers nearly every post-training method.

00:49.056 --> 00:52.351
Part two walks the stack layer by layer, with the memory math.

00:52.651 --> 00:55.394
Part three contrasts offline and online training.

00:55.694 --> 01:00.699
Part four covers where systems really differ: step time, asynchrony, consistency,

01:00.719 --> 01:03.428
and what algorithmic freedom actually means.

01:03.728 --> 01:08.186
Part five takes the frameworks apart: the shared atoms they are built from,

01:08.206 --> 01:10.037
the trade-offs between recipes,

01:10.057 --> 01:14.217
and the exact address of every term of the objective in each codebase.

01:14.517 --> 01:19.596
Part six turns it all into a data contract, an architecture, a decision procedure,

01:19.616 --> 01:21.213
and a validation protocol.

01:22.883 --> 01:23.856
Start with the math.

01:24.156 --> 01:27.689
Almost every post-training method can be written as one gradient.

01:27.989 --> 01:32.311
Sample a prompt x and a response y from some data distribution mu,

01:32.331 --> 01:37.443
then push up the log-probability of each token, weighted by a coefficient psi.

01:37.743 --> 01:42.499
Methods differ in only two places: where mu comes from, and how psi is computed.

01:43.369 --> 01:46.159
Supervised fine-tuning uses a fixed dataset,

01:46.179 --> 01:49.033
and psi is one on the masked response tokens.

01:49.333 --> 01:52.170
DPO also uses fixed data, preference pairs,

01:52.190 --> 01:58.016
and psi is a sigmoid-weighted push up on the chosen answer and down on the rejected one.

01:58.316 --> 02:03.850
PPO and GRPO sample from the policy itself and weight tokens by a clipped

02:03.870 --> 02:06.589
importance ratio times an advantage.

02:06.889 --> 02:11.834
On-policy distillation samples from the student and uses the teacher-minus-student

02:11.854 --> 02:13.650
log-probability as the signal.

02:14.520 --> 02:17.768
This one observation organizes the whole tool landscape.

02:18.068 --> 02:22.554
If mu is a fixed dataset, post-training is a trainer problem: load data,

02:22.574 --> 02:24.869
compute the loss, step the optimizer.

02:25.169 --> 02:28.751
If mu is the policy itself, it becomes a systems problem:

02:28.771 --> 02:33.365
you need a generation engine, reward computation, weight synchronization,

02:33.385 --> 02:36.778
and bookkeeping for which policy produced which token.

02:37.078 --> 02:41.050
That is why RL frameworks look so different from fine-tuning libraries.

02:42.720 --> 02:43.534
Now the stack.

02:43.834 --> 02:47.803
The source-code review organizes the ecosystem into six layers.

02:48.103 --> 02:53.195
On top is the algorithm layer: rewards, advantages, losses, critics, teachers,

02:53.215 --> 02:54.309
and update rules.

02:54.609 --> 02:56.029
That's where your research lives.

02:56.329 --> 03:00.254
Below it is the data and runtime layer: rollouts, environments,

03:00.274 --> 03:04.887
multi-turn trajectories, buffers, resource placement, and policy versions.

03:05.187 --> 03:09.179
This is what verl, OpenRLHF, SkyRL, AReaL, Miles,

03:09.199 --> 03:12.208
ROLL and NeMo RL actually compete on.

03:13.078 --> 03:19.235
Next is model execution: forward and backward passes, state sharding, and tensor,

03:19.255 --> 03:24.650
pipeline, expert and context parallelism, provided by FSDP2, DeepSpeed,

03:24.670 --> 03:26.786
Megatron-Core or TorchTitan.

03:27.086 --> 03:29.770
Beside it, but separate, is the generation layer.

03:30.070 --> 03:35.917
vLLM and SGLang handle batching, the KV cache, sampling, and hot weight updates.

03:36.217 --> 03:37.823
They never run a backward pass.

03:38.693 --> 03:43.770
At the bottom are parameterization and kernels, such as LoRA, quantization,

03:43.790 --> 03:49.275
fused losses and attention kernels, and the data quality and observability layer:

03:49.295 --> 03:53.488
data processing, verifiers, evaluation, logging and profiling.

03:54.358 --> 03:58.449
Two more layers sit beside this stack: agent and environment adapters,

03:58.469 --> 04:01.033
and hosted training services such as Tinker.

04:01.333 --> 04:04.845
Easy-to-use toolchains like LLaMA-Factory, ms-swift,

04:04.865 --> 04:08.513
Axolotl and Unsloth cut across several layers at once.

04:08.813 --> 04:11.327
The first rule of composition follows directly.

04:11.627 --> 04:14.301
A training backend cannot replace a rollout loop,

04:14.321 --> 04:16.995
and an inference engine cannot replace a trainer.

04:17.295 --> 04:20.482
Pick one component per role, then verify the whole path.

04:22.152 --> 04:25.073
Let's walk the layers with math, starting near the bottom.

04:25.373 --> 04:30.942
LoRA freezes the pretrained weight W-zero and learns a low-rank update: B times A,

04:30.962 --> 04:32.510
scaled by alpha over r.

04:32.810 --> 04:36.461
B starts at zero, so training begins exactly at the base model.

04:36.761 --> 04:40.904
Trainable parameters drop from d times k to r times d-plus-k.

04:41.204 --> 04:44.319
For a 4096 by 4096 projection at rank 8,

04:44.339 --> 04:50.822
that's about sixty-five thousand parameters instead of sixteen point eight million:

04:50.842 --> 04:52.389
roughly 0.4 percent.

04:53.259 --> 04:55.974
Why this matters becomes clear from memory accounting.

04:56.274 --> 05:02.264
Full fine-tuning with mixed-precision Adam stores about sixteen bytes per parameter:

05:02.284 --> 05:05.126
two for bf16 weights, two for gradients,

05:05.146 --> 05:09.491
and twelve for the fp32 master copy and the two Adam moments.

05:09.791 --> 05:15.434
A 7-billion-parameter model needs about 112 gigabytes before activations.

05:15.734 --> 05:19.269
With LoRA, the frozen base costs about two bytes per parameter.

05:19.569 --> 05:25.208
QLoRA stores the base in 4-bit NormalFloat, about half a byte per parameter,

05:25.228 --> 05:31.687
which is how the QLoRA paper fine-tuned a 65-billion model on a single 48-gigabyte GPU.

05:32.557 --> 05:36.100
In tools: PEFT injects adapters with get_peft_model,

05:36.120 --> 05:40.279
bitsandbytes supplies the 4-bit storage and 8-bit optimizers,

05:40.299 --> 05:43.499
and Unsloth fuses kernels for memory and speed.

05:43.799 --> 05:48.354
One engineering caveat: training only LoRA does not mean syncing only LoRA.

05:48.654 --> 05:52.505
In online RL, some integrations merge the adapter into the base,

05:52.525 --> 05:56.860
or gather the full weights, before pushing them to the inference engine.

05:58.530 --> 06:00.158
One layer up is execution,

06:00.178 --> 06:03.897
and ZeRO's analysis is the cleanest way to reason about it.

06:04.197 --> 06:08.566
Let psi be the parameter count, K the optimizer bytes per parameter,

06:08.586 --> 06:12.310
twelve for mixed-precision Adam, and N the number of GPUs.

06:12.610 --> 06:17.137
Plain data parallelism replicates everything: sixteen psi per GPU.

06:17.437 --> 06:22.742
ZeRO stage one shards the optimizer states, stage two also shards gradients,

06:22.762 --> 06:27.928
and stage three, which is also what FSDP2 does, shards the parameters too.

06:28.228 --> 06:36.748
For a 7.5-billion model on 64 GPUs, that's 120 gigabytes per GPU, then 31, then 17,

06:36.768 --> 06:38.806
and finally under 2.

06:39.676 --> 06:41.463
Three backends implement this layer.

06:41.763 --> 06:47.995
FSDP2 shards each parameter as a DTensor and keeps ordinary PyTorch control flow.

06:48.295 --> 06:55.065
DeepSpeed adds offload to CPU and NVMe, plus AutoTP, AutoEP and its hybrid engine.

06:55.365 --> 06:58.120
Megatron-Core brings tensor, pipeline,

06:58.140 --> 07:03.011
expert and context parallelism for very large dense and MoE models.

07:03.311 --> 07:05.254
Each has a contract you must respect.

07:05.554 --> 07:09.132
In FSDP2, the local shard is not the full tensor.

07:09.432 --> 07:13.226
In DeepSpeed, the engine owns gradient-accumulation boundaries.

07:13.526 --> 07:16.728
In Megatron, the pipeline schedule owns the backward pass.

07:17.598 --> 07:18.769
Composition rules.

07:19.069 --> 07:24.163
FSDP2 and ZeRO-3 are alternative ways to manage the same model's state,

07:24.183 --> 07:26.684
so don't wrap one inside the other.

07:26.984 --> 07:31.060
Ray orchestrates processes and resources, while NCCL moves tensors;

07:31.360 --> 07:32.818
they live at different layers.

07:33.118 --> 07:34.104
And a classic bug:

07:34.124 --> 07:38.912
dividing the loss by the accumulation steps yourself while the engine scales it again,

07:38.932 --> 07:40.869
which shrinks your gradients twice.

07:41.739 --> 07:43.548
And a correction worth stating plainly.

07:43.848 --> 07:48.341
DeepSpeed, DeepSpeed-Chat and OpenRLHF are three different things.

07:48.641 --> 07:51.028
DeepSpeed is execution infrastructure.

07:51.328 --> 07:56.044
DeepSpeed-Chat is one classic three-stage RLHF application built on it.

07:56.344 --> 08:00.403
OpenRLHF is a Ray pipeline that still trains with DeepSpeed.

08:00.703 --> 08:04.012
With identical math, sharding changes the cost of training,

08:04.032 --> 08:05.817
not the objective being learned.

08:07.487 --> 08:09.727
The generation layer has different physics.

08:10.027 --> 08:13.478
Prefill processes the prompt in parallel and is compute-bound.

08:13.778 --> 08:15.696
Decode emits one token per step,

08:15.716 --> 08:19.875
and every step must re-read the weights and the KV cache from memory,

08:19.895 --> 08:21.328
so it's bandwidth-bound.

08:21.628 --> 08:26.145
That's why engines like vLLM and SGLang exist: paged KV cache,

08:26.165 --> 08:32.073
continuous batching and prefix sharing let many sequences share each weight read.

08:32.373 --> 08:34.017
With long reasoning traces,

08:34.037 --> 08:38.393
this autoregressive phase is frequently the largest part of an RL step.

08:39.263 --> 08:43.817
The trainer and the engine also lay out the same weights differently:

08:43.837 --> 08:46.933
FSDP shards or Megatron partitions on one side,

08:46.953 --> 08:49.717
inference tensor parallelism on the other.

08:50.017 --> 08:54.389
So every policy update means resharding, possibly converting precision,

08:54.409 --> 08:56.059
transferring and reloading.

08:56.359 --> 08:58.704
vLLM now separates the weight source,

08:58.724 --> 09:02.857
the trainer-side transfer engine and the inference-side receiver.

09:03.157 --> 09:07.548
SGLang supports updates from disk, tensors or a distributed group,

09:07.568 --> 09:09.018
with pause and resume.

09:09.318 --> 09:10.644
Weight sync is not plumbing;

09:10.944 --> 09:12.279
it's a protocol boundary.

09:13.149 --> 09:14.155
A practical rule:

09:14.175 --> 09:20.434
anything the policy needs in order to generate must be representable in the engine and synced correctly.

09:20.734 --> 09:22.544
Learner-only parts like critics,

09:22.564 --> 09:25.803
Q-heads or target networks can stay on the training side.

09:26.103 --> 09:30.707
DeepSpeed's hybrid engine was an early answer to this tension: the same actor,

09:30.727 --> 09:34.264
switching between a training layout and a generation layout.

09:35.934 --> 09:38.050
Part three: offline versus online.

09:38.350 --> 09:40.477
Offline methods train on a fixed dataset.

09:40.777 --> 09:43.901
SFT minimizes masked negative log-likelihood.

09:44.201 --> 09:51.004
DPO turns a preference pair into a logistic loss on the difference of policy-to-reference log-ratios,

09:51.024 --> 09:52.018
scaled by beta.

09:52.318 --> 09:54.330
Neither needs a generation engine,

09:54.350 --> 09:58.813
and the frozen reference model's log-probabilities can even be precomputed.

09:59.683 --> 10:01.791
The subtle part is the normalizer Z.

10:02.091 --> 10:04.867
It looks like a detail, but it's part of the objective.

10:05.167 --> 10:07.452
Token-mean gives every token the same weight.

10:07.752 --> 10:11.200
Sequence-mean averages within each response first,

10:11.220 --> 10:17.512
so a token in a 1000-token answer weighs ten times less than a token in a 100-token answer.

10:17.812 --> 10:20.770
And with gradient accumulation or multiple ranks,

10:20.790 --> 10:23.748
an average of local means is not the global mean.

10:24.048 --> 10:25.295
These aren't system knobs;

10:25.595 --> 10:27.003
they change what you optimize.

10:27.873 --> 10:34.062
The standard offline composition is: Transformers for the model, PEFT for adapters,

10:34.082 --> 10:39.748
bitsandbytes when memory is tight, Accelerate, DeepSpeed or FSDP2 for scale,

10:39.768 --> 10:44.311
and TRL's SFT or DPO trainer for the objective and lifecycle.

10:44.611 --> 10:45.603
LLaMA-Factory,

10:45.623 --> 10:50.662
ms-swift and Axolotl package the same pieces as config-driven recipes,

10:50.682 --> 10:53.554
and Unsloth focuses on memory and speed.

10:54.424 --> 10:59.412
One efficiency boundary from the code review: in the TRL snapshot reviewed,

10:59.432 --> 11:03.619
a custom compute-loss function runs a full-logits forward pass,

11:03.639 --> 11:06.891
plus an extra no-gradient fused pass for metrics,

11:06.911 --> 11:10.029
while the default path can use a fused LM head.

11:10.329 --> 11:11.870
So when you change the loss,

11:11.890 --> 11:15.548
re-check memory and throughput instead of inheriting the defaults.

11:17.218 --> 11:18.859
Online RL closes a loop.

11:19.159 --> 11:22.763
Sample prompts, generate rollouts with the inference engine,

11:22.783 --> 11:27.413
score them with a reward model, verifier or environment, estimate advantages,

11:27.433 --> 11:31.400
update the learner, publish new weights to the engine, and repeat.

11:31.700 --> 11:35.781
Every arrow in this loop is a place where a framework makes design decisions.

11:36.652 --> 11:38.097
The objectives are compact.

11:38.397 --> 11:40.684
PPO maximizes a clipped surrogate:

11:40.704 --> 11:45.978
the probability ratio between the current and old policy, times the advantage,

11:45.998 --> 11:48.489
clipped to one plus or minus epsilon.

11:48.789 --> 11:50.651
GRPO removes the critic.

11:50.951 --> 11:56.172
It samples a group of G responses per prompt and standardizes each reward against

11:56.192 --> 11:58.630
the group mean and standard deviation,

11:58.650 --> 12:02.059
with an optional KL penalty toward a reference model.

12:02.929 --> 12:05.261
Here's what separates careful implementations.

12:05.561 --> 12:07.315
There are four probability tracks.

12:07.615 --> 12:11.842
Mu is the behavior distribution the engine actually sampled from.

12:12.142 --> 12:15.763
Pi-old is the trainer's recomputation at the start of the step.

12:16.063 --> 12:18.732
Pi-theta is the current, differentiable policy.

12:19.032 --> 12:22.539
And pi-ref is the KL anchor, or a teacher in distillation.

12:22.839 --> 12:28.839
The true importance weight factorizes into the PPO ratio times a train-inference mismatch term.

12:29.139 --> 12:32.813
OpenRLHF, AReaL and others keep these tracks separate,

12:32.833 --> 12:36.712
and truncated importance sampling caps the mismatch term.

12:38.382 --> 12:40.650
Part four is where systems really differ.

12:40.950 --> 12:44.646
Write down the time of one synchronous step: generation, reward,

12:44.666 --> 12:47.085
training and weight sync, all in sequence.

12:47.385 --> 12:49.793
With pipelining on independent resources,

12:49.813 --> 12:54.530
the ideal step time approaches the slower of generation and training, plus sync.

12:54.830 --> 12:56.556
Amdahl's law sets the priority.

12:56.856 --> 12:59.330
If generation is 80 percent of the step,

12:59.350 --> 13:02.635
making training twice as fast saves only ten percent.

13:03.505 --> 13:07.348
Published numbers confirm that async is not automatically faster.

13:07.648 --> 13:10.475
In NeMo RL's performance table,

13:10.495 --> 13:19.201
DeepSeek-V3 on 512 H100s dropped from 111 to about 64 seconds per step with one-step-off async.

13:19.501 --> 13:25.919
But Qwen3 30B-A3B on 16 GB200s went from 221 to 301 seconds:

13:25.939 --> 13:29.675
splitting resources made it slower.

13:29.975 --> 13:35.468
And Miles cut a trillion-parameter Kimi K2 weight update from 53 seconds with NCCL

13:35.488 --> 13:39.234
broadcast to about 7 seconds with peer-to-peer transfer,

13:39.254 --> 13:42.999
which shows weight sync can be a first-class bottleneck.

13:43.869 --> 13:49.243
So the metric that matters is cost to target: total GPU-hours, across all roles,

13:49.263 --> 13:51.603
to reach the same validation score.

13:51.903 --> 13:55.452
Tokens per second can reward long, useless generations;

13:55.752 --> 13:58.073
steps per second can hide smaller batches;

13:58.373 --> 14:00.403
wall time can hide extra GPUs.

14:00.703 --> 14:03.520
Profile each phase, and fix the biggest one first.

14:05.190 --> 14:06.936
Asynchrony introduces staleness.

14:07.236 --> 14:12.546
Define a sample's age as the learner's current version minus the version that generated it.

14:12.846 --> 14:17.661
With mid-flight weight updates, one trajectory can even span several versions,

14:17.681 --> 14:21.319
so the version belongs to token spans, not just to samples.

14:21.619 --> 14:26.343
A strict gate only consumes data with age at most k, and importance weights,

14:26.363 --> 14:29.276
usually truncated, correct the remaining shift.

14:30.146 --> 14:33.801
The key insight from the code review is that async is not a boolean.

14:34.101 --> 14:36.948
A bounded queue limits how far producers run ahead,

14:36.968 --> 14:39.872
but not the age of each sample when it's trained on.

14:40.172 --> 14:43.045
An admission window decides whether new tasks start,

14:43.065 --> 14:45.770
but a slow in-flight task can still finish stale.

14:46.070 --> 14:48.430
A consume-time gate filters by version,

14:48.450 --> 14:51.541
but dropping samples changes the data distribution.

14:51.841 --> 14:56.649
Importance correction fixes known probability shifts, but not for free in variance.

14:56.949 --> 15:01.296
And mid-flight updates buy overlap at the cost of mixed-policy trajectories.

15:02.166 --> 15:04.448
Real frameworks implement different subsets.

15:04.748 --> 15:07.623
OpenRLHF mainly uses queue back-pressure.

15:07.923 --> 15:11.708
SkyRL's staleness setting governs admission and capacity.

15:12.008 --> 15:17.357
Miles and prime-rl can filter by age at consumption, and verl offers drop or wait.

15:17.657 --> 15:21.524
A staleness value of two may not mean the same age in two frameworks.

15:21.824 --> 15:24.001
And if long trajectories are discarded,

15:24.021 --> 15:26.593
hard problems quietly disappear from training.

15:26.893 --> 15:29.632
Measure age, length and difficulty explicitly.

15:31.302 --> 15:36.400
Consistency between training and inference has at least five independent sources of error.

15:36.700 --> 15:42.316
Re-tokenizing text can change the token sequence, so store the exact generated IDs.

15:42.616 --> 15:46.778
The behavior policy may be stale, so record versions and correct for it.

15:47.078 --> 15:50.318
MoE routers may pick different experts in the trainer,

15:50.338 --> 15:52.371
so replay the rollout-time routes.

15:52.671 --> 15:55.904
Top-p or top-k sampling truncates the distribution.

15:56.204 --> 15:57.282
And different kernels,

15:57.302 --> 16:01.022
precision or batch shapes produce different log-probs for the same weights.

16:01.322 --> 16:03.259
Each mechanism fixes one source;

16:03.559 --> 16:05.090
none substitutes for another.

16:05.960 --> 16:08.167
The top-p case is a clean piece of math.

16:08.467 --> 16:13.826
Nucleus sampling draws from the smallest set S whose probability mass reaches p,

16:13.846 --> 16:15.170
renormalized by Z_S.

16:15.470 --> 16:18.948
So the true behavior log-prob is log pi minus log Z_S.

16:19.248 --> 16:22.741
If the trainer recomputes a full-vocabulary softmax,

16:22.761 --> 16:27.267
every ratio is off by a factor Z_S that varies from token to token.

16:27.567 --> 16:33.117
Sampler-support replay fixes this by normalizing over the same candidate set during training.

16:34.787 --> 16:38.318
Now, what does it mean to customize every detail of an algorithm?

16:38.618 --> 16:43.353
There are six distinct freedoms: the objective, the estimator, the data lifetime,

16:43.373 --> 16:46.816
the model state, the update rule, and execution scheduling.

16:47.116 --> 16:48.949
Each needs a different kind of access.

16:49.249 --> 16:52.883
A custom-loss hook usually covers only part of the first row.

16:53.183 --> 16:57.197
A new critic, a target network or replay touches rows three to five,

16:57.217 --> 17:00.460
which live in the learner loop, not in a loss callback.

17:01.330 --> 17:04.204
Here's a concrete example of why the inputs matter.

17:04.504 --> 17:08.009
Exact entropy needs the full vocabulary distribution.

17:08.309 --> 17:13.353
If your hook only receives the log-prob of the sampled token, the naive move,

17:13.373 --> 17:18.155
adding minus log pi of that token as a bonus, has zero expected gradient,

17:18.175 --> 17:22.496
because the score function has mean zero under on-policy sampling.

17:22.796 --> 17:26.208
The correct estimator weights the score by the log-prob itself.

17:26.508 --> 17:28.792
And exact entropy needs full logits,

17:28.812 --> 17:32.953
which under tensor parallelism are sharded across the vocabulary.

17:34.623 --> 17:38.190
With those freedoms in mind, here's where each framework lets you in,

17:38.210 --> 17:39.854
based on the pinned source code.

17:40.154 --> 17:43.425
TRL gives you trainer subclasses and loss functions,

17:43.445 --> 17:46.715
but you inherit the upstream Transformers lifecycle.

17:47.015 --> 17:51.904
SkyRL has separate registries for policy losses and advantage estimators,

17:51.924 --> 17:54.123
plus a clean generator interface.

17:54.423 --> 17:58.514
slime's custom loss receives differentiable logits and a reducer,

17:58.534 --> 18:00.159
which is unusually direct.

18:00.459 --> 18:03.490
verl's registries pass selected-token log-probs,

18:03.510 --> 18:06.796
so full-logit objectives need deeper worker changes.

18:07.096 --> 18:09.873
ROLL lets you override the worker's loss on logits.

18:10.173 --> 18:14.618
And OpenRLHF is a few explicit files that are easy to fork.

18:15.488 --> 18:18.504
NeMo RL has a design worth copying everywhere:

18:18.524 --> 18:23.453
its loss interface receives the global count of valid tokens and sequences,

18:23.473 --> 18:29.657
so distributed normalization is part of the contract instead of an accident of micro-batching.

18:29.957 --> 18:33.596
The right comparison is not which framework lists more algorithms.

18:33.896 --> 18:38.173
It's how many boundaries your change must cross, which fast paths survive it,

18:38.193 --> 18:39.847
and what it costs to maintain.

18:41.517 --> 18:43.208
Here is the landscape at a glance.

18:43.508 --> 18:46.955
These are judgments about architecture fit, not a speed ranking.

18:47.256 --> 18:49.155
verl is the broadest platform,

18:49.175 --> 18:53.315
with HybridFlow separating control flow from distributed compute.

18:53.615 --> 19:00.685
OpenRLHF composes Ray, DeepSpeed and vLLM in a way HF teams find natural.

19:00.985 --> 19:06.354
slime is a thin algorithm layer on Megatron and SGLang, strong for large MoE.

19:06.654 --> 19:11.403
Miles, forked from slime, invests in weight transfer, consistency and recovery.

19:11.703 --> 19:15.986
SkyRL offers modular generator and environment interfaces.

19:16.286 --> 19:19.572
AReaL co-designs asynchronous sampling with the learning rule.

19:19.872 --> 19:24.103
NeMo RL integrates NVIDIA's large-model stack and distillation.

19:24.403 --> 19:27.652
ROLL handles multi-domain, multi-reward pipelines.

19:27.952 --> 19:30.707
RLinf targets embodied and VLA training,

19:30.727 --> 19:35.079
and Prime-RL couples async training with verifier environments.

19:35.949 --> 19:39.828
Around them: fine-tuning toolchains for SFT and preference work;

19:40.128 --> 19:42.821
agent adapters that capture real agent traces;

19:43.121 --> 19:44.124
environment hubs;

19:44.424 --> 19:45.086
and Tinker,

19:45.106 --> 19:50.046
a hosted API where you write the loop and the service runs distributed training.

19:50.346 --> 19:51.934
Also check maintenance status.

19:52.234 --> 19:57.850
torchtune is no longer actively developed, ReaLHF is archived in favor of AReaL,

19:57.870 --> 20:01.303
and TorchForge's direction moved into TorchTitan.

20:02.973 --> 20:06.966
Zoom out, and the frameworks stop looking like a dozen unrelated systems.

20:07.266 --> 20:10.066
They are recipes built from a handful of shared atoms.

20:10.366 --> 20:15.975
At the bottom, everyone shares PyTorch, torch dot distributed with NCCL for tensors,

20:15.995 --> 20:17.718
and the same CUDA kernels.

20:18.018 --> 20:22.417
Above that, every recipe picks one training backbone, FSDP2,

20:22.437 --> 20:24.774
DeepSpeed ZeRO or Megatron-Core;

20:25.074 --> 20:28.357
one generation engine, vLLM or SGLang;

20:28.657 --> 20:30.499
and an orchestrator, usually Ray.

20:31.369 --> 20:32.663
Now read the recipes.

20:32.963 --> 20:37.380
OpenRLHF is Ray, plus DeepSpeed ZeRO, plus vLLM.

20:37.680 --> 20:39.692
slime is Megatron plus SGLang.

20:39.992 --> 20:44.006
SkyRL is FSDP2 or Megatron, plus vLLM.

20:44.306 --> 20:48.150
verl, ROLL and NeMo RL ship both backbones and both engines.

20:48.450 --> 20:49.514
The atoms repeat;

20:49.814 --> 20:54.042
what each framework really adds is the last column, its connective layer:

20:54.062 --> 20:57.883
the trajectory format, the control loop, and the hooks it exposes.

20:58.183 --> 21:01.032
That layer decides what you can change, and how cheaply.

21:01.902 --> 21:04.393
One warning before combining atoms yourself.

21:04.693 --> 21:10.082
A recipe is a path that is already connected end to end: model, training backend,

21:10.102 --> 21:12.887
weight export, engine, trajectory, update.

21:13.187 --> 21:15.073
Feature checkboxes do not add up.

21:15.373 --> 21:18.417
The source review found concrete holes:

21:18.437 --> 21:22.267
OpenRLHF's Ray plus vLLM online path has no LoRA;

21:22.567 --> 21:26.037
Miles' FSDP2 backend is data-parallel only;

21:26.337 --> 21:29.423
NeMo RL's async replay path excludes SGLang;

21:29.723 --> 21:33.623
and TRL's async trainer runs on FSDP2, not ZeRO.

21:35.293 --> 21:36.612
Why do recipes differ?

21:36.912 --> 21:39.395
Each one is a point on four trade-off axes.

21:39.695 --> 21:41.515
The first is the training backbone.

21:41.815 --> 21:46.852
FSDP2 keeps your PyTorch model and control flow intact, but leaves rollout,

21:46.872 --> 21:48.470
critics and sync to you.

21:48.770 --> 21:51.334
DeepSpeed ZeRO shards states and offloads,

21:51.354 --> 21:54.717
but its engine owns accumulation and the step boundary.

21:55.017 --> 22:00.599
Megatron-Core brings tensor, pipeline, expert and context parallelism for large MoE,

22:00.619 --> 22:05.401
at the price of model mapping and a schedule that reaches into training.

22:05.701 --> 22:08.640
The second axis is the generation engine:

22:08.660 --> 22:14.702
vLLM with its native weight-transfer engines, or SGLang with update sessions, pause,

22:14.722 --> 22:16.073
and memory release.

22:16.943 --> 22:18.487
The third axis is placement.

22:18.787 --> 22:22.832
Colocated and synchronous keeps the policy fresh with fewer copies;

22:23.132 --> 22:27.504
separated and asynchronous hides the long tail, but adds staleness,

22:27.524 --> 22:31.110
weight transfer and correction, and it is not monotone:

22:31.130 --> 22:35.961
NeMo RL's own table shows async faster on one model and slower on another.

22:36.261 --> 22:38.464
The fourth axis is the connective layer.

22:38.764 --> 22:41.289
A thin one, like slime or OpenRLHF,

22:41.309 --> 22:45.796
hands your hook real tensors but exposes the backend directly.

22:46.096 --> 22:49.863
A platform, like verl or ROLL, carries many backends and roles,

22:49.883 --> 22:53.951
but you learn its abstractions before your learning rule is visible.

22:55.621 --> 22:57.646
So far, frameworks as recipes.

22:57.946 --> 23:00.679
Now let's go the other way, from the math to the code.

23:00.979 --> 23:02.873
Write out the full online objective,

23:02.893 --> 23:05.424
and number every place you might want to change.

23:05.724 --> 23:10.415
Positions one to six are the data rule: which prompts, who generates, the reward,

23:10.435 --> 23:14.485
how long data lives, how stale it may be, and the behavior correction.

23:14.785 --> 23:18.062
They decide which samples enter the gradient, and with what weight.

23:18.932 --> 23:22.869
Positions seven to twelve are the learning rule: the advantage,

23:22.889 --> 23:27.579
the surrogate on sampled tokens, anything that needs the full distribution,

23:27.599 --> 23:31.473
the normalization, extra networks, and the update rule itself.

23:31.773 --> 23:35.278
Thirteen closes the loop by publishing weights back to the engine.

23:35.578 --> 23:39.117
Notice that the two halves are coupled through position six:

23:39.137 --> 23:41.963
the moment a data rule reuses or delays samples,

23:41.983 --> 23:46.885
the learning rule must correct for the behavior policy that actually produced them.

23:48.555 --> 23:52.332
Here is the address book: thirteen positions against eleven routes,

23:52.352 --> 23:54.712
each cell colored by how deep you must go.

23:55.012 --> 23:56.638
L zero is a config flag.

23:56.938 --> 23:58.393
L one is a declared hook.

23:58.693 --> 24:03.226
L two means subclassing a stage, L three means editing framework files,

24:03.246 --> 24:07.395
and L four means the change crosses worker or backend boundaries.

24:07.695 --> 24:10.121
Dashed cells were not covered by the source review.

24:10.421 --> 24:11.379
Read it by rows.

24:11.679 --> 24:16.765
Generation and staleness, rows two and five, are config or hooks almost everywhere:

24:16.785 --> 24:19.348
this is what these runtimes are built for.

24:20.218 --> 24:26.466
Rows seven and eight, advantage and surrogate, are hooks in verl, SkyRL, slime,

24:26.486 --> 24:31.147
NeMo RL and prime-rl, but file edits in OpenRLHF and Miles.

24:31.447 --> 24:37.467
Row nine splits the field: slime and NeMo RL hand your loss the logits, while verl,

24:37.487 --> 24:41.398
SkyRL and prime-rl pass only selected-token log-probs,

24:41.418 --> 24:44.673
so an exact entropy becomes an L four change.

24:44.973 --> 24:48.720
And rows eleven and twelve, new networks and new update rules,

24:48.740 --> 24:50.483
are L four nearly everywhere.

24:50.783 --> 24:54.823
That pattern is the decision rule: pick the route where your change is shallowest;

24:55.123 --> 24:58.893
if it is L four on every route, own the learner and reuse the runtime.

25:00.563 --> 25:02.955
Let's trace three concrete changes through the book.

25:03.255 --> 25:08.286
First, a data rule: prioritize hard prompts and reuse each group up to k times.

25:08.586 --> 25:11.470
That touches positions one, four, five and six.

25:11.770 --> 25:16.923
The shallowest routes are Miles' replaceable data buffer and verl's custom replay buffer.

25:17.223 --> 25:18.622
Write the rule as put and get;

25:18.922 --> 25:20.143
gate age at consumption;

25:20.443 --> 25:23.251
and remember that a reused sample is off-policy,

25:23.271 --> 25:26.314
so it must carry its behavior log-probs and version.

25:26.614 --> 25:31.139
Note that the default buffers in Miles and ROLL delete samples once consumed.

25:32.009 --> 25:36.964
Second, a learning rule that needs the full distribution: an exact entropy bonus.

25:37.264 --> 25:38.828
That is positions nine and ten.

25:39.128 --> 25:41.212
Your hook must receive logits,

25:41.232 --> 25:46.824
because a naive minus log-pi bonus on sampled tokens has zero expected gradient.

25:47.124 --> 25:49.814
slime, NeMo RL and ROLL hand you logits;

25:50.114 --> 25:55.135
under tensor parallelism they are vocabulary-sharded, so use vocab-parallel ops,

25:55.155 --> 25:59.357
and reduce with the framework's reducer instead of averaging twice.

26:00.227 --> 26:04.474
Third, new state: a Q-critic with a target network, its own optimizer,

26:04.494 --> 26:06.120
and four updates per batch.

26:06.420 --> 26:11.324
Positions eleven, twelve and thirteen, and every framework column says L four.

26:11.624 --> 26:17.667
So own the learner: create the models and optimizers yourself on FSDP2 or DeepSpeed,

26:17.687 --> 26:22.575
respect their step rules, publish only the policy to vLLM or SGLang,

26:22.595 --> 26:28.348
and checkpoint every optimizer, the target, the buffer position and the version.

26:30.018 --> 26:31.052
So what should you own?

26:31.352 --> 26:34.338
The review's answer is: own the trajectory contract.

26:34.638 --> 26:36.571
Keep exact token IDs per turn.

26:36.871 --> 26:39.389
Separate attention, loss and packing masks.

26:39.689 --> 26:43.357
Store behavior log-probs together with the sampling parameters.

26:43.657 --> 26:46.548
Keep old, reference and teacher log-probs distinct.

26:46.848 --> 26:49.226
Carry group, episode and turn IDs.

26:49.526 --> 26:51.314
Record versions per token span.

26:51.614 --> 26:55.790
And distinguish terminated from truncated, because they bootstrap differently.

26:56.090 --> 27:00.638
When you switch backends, this contract tells you exactly what information was lost.

27:01.508 --> 27:06.738
Then keep a minimal architecture of six modules: trajectory, algorithm, learner,

27:06.758 --> 27:08.838
rollout, runtime and evaluation.

27:09.138 --> 27:13.947
Write the algorithm in plain PyTorch, and reuse engines and runtimes for the rest.

27:14.247 --> 27:19.454
This loop makes the control flow explicit: collect, encode, plan updates, sample,

27:19.474 --> 27:23.714
forward, compute the objective, step, update targets, and publish.

27:24.014 --> 27:26.763
If your method is a single-actor policy gradient,

27:26.783 --> 27:29.476
hand most of these steps to an existing trainer.

27:29.776 --> 27:32.315
If it has multiple networks, own the learner loop.

27:33.185 --> 27:34.605
Finally, name your clocks.

27:34.905 --> 27:35.902
An optimizer step,

27:35.922 --> 27:40.588
a published policy version and a recoverable checkpoint are three different events.

27:40.888 --> 27:45.284
And a real checkpoint for online training also needs the rollout queue,

27:45.304 --> 27:49.887
trajectory versions, replay position, environment state and sampler state,

27:49.907 --> 27:51.317
not just model weights.

27:52.987 --> 27:55.781
Now we can turn all of this into a decision procedure.

27:56.081 --> 27:59.620
First question: does your training data come from the current policy?

27:59.920 --> 28:01.079
If not, you're offline.

28:01.379 --> 28:03.106
Start with TRL plus PEFT;

28:03.406 --> 28:06.091
add DeepSpeed or FSDP2 for scale;

28:06.391 --> 28:11.205
use LLaMA-Factory, ms-swift or Axolotl for config-driven recipes;

28:11.505 --> 28:14.501
QLoRA or Unsloth when memory is the constraint;

28:14.801 --> 28:16.174
and Tunix on TPUs.

28:17.044 --> 28:21.083
If you're online, ask: does your idea fit inside the generator,

28:21.103 --> 28:23.467
advantage and policy-loss interfaces?

28:23.767 --> 28:25.773
If yes, reuse a complete runtime.

28:26.073 --> 28:31.536
The review's default first prototype is SkyRL on FSDP2 with vLLM;

28:31.836 --> 28:34.552
verl for a shared, multi-backend team platform;

28:34.852 --> 28:39.261
OpenRLHF if you already have HF and DeepSpeed assets;

28:39.561 --> 28:44.329
slime, Miles or NeMo RL once you've committed to Megatron and large MoE.

28:44.629 --> 28:50.105
If not, because you need new networks, replay or a different update rule,

28:50.125 --> 28:51.309
own the learner,

28:51.329 --> 28:57.106
and reuse FSDP2 or DeepSpeed for execution and vLLM or SGLang for generation.

28:57.976 --> 28:59.167
Specialized cases.

28:59.467 --> 29:04.664
If you already have a working agent, keep its harness and capture traces with rLLM,

29:04.684 --> 29:06.110
Agent Lightning or ART.

29:06.410 --> 29:10.303
If asynchrony itself is your research, study AReaL and PipelineRL.

29:10.603 --> 29:14.073
For embodied or VLA training, evaluate RLinf.

29:14.373 --> 29:19.242
And if you'd rather not run GPUs at all, Tinker exposes forward-backward,

29:19.262 --> 29:23.662
optimizer step and sampling as an API, within LoRA-centric limits.

29:25.332 --> 29:26.686
How do you validate a choice?

29:26.986 --> 29:28.016
Use two stages.

29:28.316 --> 29:33.587
Stage one fixes the trajectories: feed every candidate the same tokens, masks,

29:33.607 --> 29:37.657
rewards and behavior log-probs, and compare loss, gradients,

29:37.677 --> 29:40.574
a single parameter update, memory and time.

29:40.874 --> 29:44.449
That separates execution differences from sampling differences.

29:44.749 --> 29:49.152
Stage two runs the closed loop with the same start checkpoint, data,

29:49.172 --> 29:51.949
evaluation and budget, aligning every knob,

29:51.969 --> 29:55.982
from group size and sampling parameters to loss reduction, KL,

29:56.002 --> 29:57.478
clipping and filtering.

29:57.778 --> 30:01.155
Then compare GPU-hours to target across several seeds.

30:02.025 --> 30:04.337
And when reading code, check these questions,

30:04.357 --> 30:07.290
which matter more than the number of built-in algorithms.

30:07.590 --> 30:11.514
Where exactly is the final mean taken, and does the engine scale again?

30:11.814 --> 30:14.273
Are group statistics aggregated across ranks?

30:14.573 --> 30:17.078
Are top-p log-probs normalized on the support?

30:17.378 --> 30:19.719
Does any branch silently detach a tensor?

30:20.019 --> 30:24.168
Does weight sync push everything, run quantization post-processing,

30:24.188 --> 30:25.537
and invalidate caches?

30:25.837 --> 30:29.470
Does the buffer delete samples, and does resume restore everything?

30:30.340 --> 30:33.125
Keep a tiny pure-PyTorch reference loss and step.

30:33.425 --> 30:37.321
After every kernel, precision or backend change, compare against it.

30:37.621 --> 30:41.515
Then improve efficiency one bottleneck at a time, in profile order.

30:41.815 --> 30:45.557
It's slower on day one, and much faster over the life of a project.

30:47.227 --> 30:49.024
Let's wrap up with five takeaways.

30:49.324 --> 30:53.168
One: every post-training method is one gradient with two choices,

30:53.188 --> 30:55.368
the data source and the token weight;

30:55.668 --> 31:00.145
fixed data makes it a trainer problem, on-policy data makes it a systems problem.

31:00.445 --> 31:03.268
Two: compose by layer, one component per role,

31:03.288 --> 31:06.977
and verify the whole path before you trust a feature matrix.

31:07.277 --> 31:12.014
Three: frameworks differ less in their algorithm lists than in scheduling,

31:12.034 --> 31:15.421
consistency, MoE depth, weight transfer and recovery.

31:16.291 --> 31:18.861
Four: find your change's address in the objective.

31:19.161 --> 31:21.235
If it stays in hooks, reuse a framework;

31:21.535 --> 31:26.071
if it crosses boundaries everywhere, own the learner and the trajectory contract,

31:26.091 --> 31:27.309
and reuse the runtime.

31:27.609 --> 31:31.153
Five: optimize cost to target, one bottleneck at a time,

31:31.173 --> 31:34.527
with a small reference implementation as your anchor.

31:34.827 --> 31:35.783
Thanks for watching.
